A string literal initialises a char array member, laid out inline.

THIS IS THE DECLARATION THAT KEPT mc-tcc FROM BEING A WHOLE tcc.

    static const ArHdr arhdr_init = {
        "/               ",        char ar_name[16]
        "0           ",            char ar_date[12]
        ...
        ARFMAG                     char ar_fmag[2]   -- "`\n"
        };                                              tcctools.c:60

tcc.c includes tcctools.c, so this one declaration made tcc's own main()
uncompilable, and mc-tcc carried a 175-line hand-written driver instead --
`micro-c-libc/impl/main-tcc.c`, which knew -o -I -B -c and skipped every other
argument, and held ONE input file in a single pointer that each argument
overwrote.

Measured, once it was closed: micro-c compiles tcc.c unmodified, 707 functions
against libtcc's 695, and the linked binary answers

    $ mc-tcc --version
    tcc version 0.9.28rc (AArch64 Linux)

with -E, -c, -print-search-dirs and MULTIPLE INPUT FILES all working, because
they are tcc's code and always were. Two files in one invocation compile, link
and run.

WHY IT NEEDED NEW CODE. micro-c has a string path for globals, but only
`if(type_is_pointer(type_size))` -- it emits a POINTER to storage elsewhere. A
`char[16]` MEMBER cannot be that: the struct is memcpy'd into an archive header
and written to a file, so the bytes belong at the member's own offset.

EXACTLY member->size BYTES, AND NO NUL BY ASSUMPTION. ar_fmag is two bytes
initialised from a two-character string, which C allows precisely because the
terminator is dropped when it does not fit. Emitting the literal and letting M1
append its own NUL would make the struct one byte long and every field after it
wrong. Short strings pad with zeroes; long ones truncate. Case 104 checks all
three shapes and checks the member AFTER a string member is still at its own
offset, which is the thing a wrong byte count moves.

ESCAPES ARE DECODED HERE. ARFMAG is a backtick and a NEWLINE: three characters
of source, two bytes of data. Emitting the source text would put a backslash
and an 'n' into every archive header. It reuses escape_lookup and
amount_of_escaped_chars_to_skip -- the pair the string collector already uses --
rather than a second copy of the rule, because "one rule, several
implementations, and the copies disagree" is the class that has cost this spike
more than every missing feature put together.

IT CANNOT BE STUBBED, which is worth saying because it nearly was. The argument
for stubbing `tcc -ar` was that stage 4 borrows binutils. That is true of the
current borrow list and false of the goal: gcc needs an assembler and linker, so
binutils must be built BY tcc, and building it means creating libbfd.a and
libiberty.a before any ar exists. `tcc -ar` is the answer to that
chicken-and-egg. And zeroing the initialiser does not degrade gracefully --
ar_name is "/               " and ar_fmag is ARFMAG, both format-critical, so a
zeroed struct writes INVALID archives rather than archives with poor metadata.

Case 104. difftest aarch64 88/0 -> 89/0. The twelve end-to-end programs stay
12/12, now compiled by a tcc with its own driver.
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -6120,6 +6120,65 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/* A STRING LITERAL INITIALISING A char ARRAY MEMBER, LAID OUT INLINE.
+ *
+ * micro-c has a string path for globals, but only `if(type_is_pointer(...))`,
+ * where it emits a POINTER to storage elsewhere. A `char[16]` MEMBER cannot be
+ * that: the bytes belong at the member's own offset, because the struct is
+ * memcpy'd and written to a file and the layout has to be exact.
+ *
+ *     static const ArHdr arhdr_init = {
+ *         "/               ",        char ar_name[16]
+ *         "0           ",            char ar_date[12]
+ *         ...
+ *         ARFMAG                     char ar_fmag[2]   -- "`\n", NO room for NUL
+ *     };                                          tcctools.c:60
+ *
+ * That declaration is the only thing in tcc's whole front end micro-c could
+ * not parse, and it is why mc-tcc has a 175-line hand-written driver instead
+ * of tcc.c.
+ *
+ * EXACTLY member->size BYTES, NEVER A NUL BY ASSUMPTION. ar_fmag is two bytes
+ * initialised from a two-character string, which C allows precisely because
+ * the terminator is dropped when it does not fit. Emitting the string and
+ * letting M1 append its own NUL would make the struct one byte too long and
+ * every field after it wrong. Short strings pad with zeroes; long ones
+ * truncate, which is what C says.
+ *
+ * ESCAPES ARE DECODED HERE. ARFMAG is a backtick and a newline -- three
+ * characters of source, two bytes of data. Emitting the source text would put
+ * a backslash and an 'n' in the archive header. escape_lookup and
+ * amount_of_escaped_chars_to_skip are the same pair the string collector uses,
+ * so there is one answer to "what byte is this" rather than a second copy of
+ * the rule -- the class of bug this file has paid for nine times. */
+void global_member_string_output(struct type* member)
+{
+	char* p = global_token->s + 1;   /* past the opening quote */
+	int written = 0;
+	int ch;
+
+	while(0 != p[0])
+	{
+		if(written >= member->size) break;
+		ch = escape_lookup(p);
+		if('\\' == p[0])
+		{
+			p = p + amount_of_escaped_chars_to_skip(p);
+		}
+		p = p + 1;
+		global_value_output(ch, 1);
+		written = written + 1;
+	}
+
+	while(written < member->size)
+	{
+		global_value_output(0, 1);
+		written = written + 1;
+	}
+
+	require_extra_token();
+}
+
 void global_struct_initializer_list(struct type* type_size)
 {
 	require_match("Struct assignment initialization is invalid for globals.", "{");
@@ -6166,7 +6225,16 @@
 			cursor = cursor + 1;
 		}
 
-		global_value_selection(member->type);
+		/* An ARRAY member initialised by a STRING is laid out inline; every
+		 * other member goes through the ordinary value path. */
+		if(member->is_array && ('"' == global_token->s[0]))
+		{
+			global_member_string_output(member);
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			global_value_selection(member->type);
+		}
 		cursor = member->offset + member->size;
 
 		member = member->members;
